WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.900
Attack surface documentation is a structured record of the parts of a system that matter for security risk.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:17.480
It should describe systems, interfaces, identities, data flows, dependencies, exposed services, trust boundaries, owners, and security assumptions.

3
00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:20.940
The purpose is not to create a beautiful diagram for a slide deck.

4
00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:26.160
The purpose is to make the system easier to understand, review, defend, and maintain.

5
00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:30.280
Undocumented attack surface becomes unmanaged risk.

6
00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:41.900
When teams do not know which APIs exist, which identities can reach production data, which third parties receive information, or which services are exposed to users, they cannot reliably prioritize security work.

7
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:50.000
Vulnerability management, architecture review, incident response, and compliance all suffer when the basic shape of the system is unclear.

8
00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:59.160
Attack surface documentation is related to asset inventory, architecture documentation, and threat modeling, but it is not identical to any one of them.

9
00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:01.660
Asset inventory lists what exists.

10
00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:05.120
Architecture documentation explains how the system is designed.

11
00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:08.260
Threat modeling analyzes how things could go wrong.

12
00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:19.720
Attack surface documentation focuses on the security-relevant entry points, relationships, dependencies, permissions, flows, and assumptions that shape exposure.

13
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:29.840
AI can help by organizing evidence, summarizing source material, extracting candidate components, normalizing inconsistent names, and turning notes into structured outputs.

14
00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:33.780
But AI cannot replace source data or human validation.

15
00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:37.880
If the source material is incomplete, the output will be incomplete.

16
00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:45.500
If the model infers facts without evidence, the documentation can become more polished while becoming less trustworthy.

17
00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:49.140
A defensive workflow stays authorized and scoped.

18
00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:57.800
The team should document systems it owns or is permitted to assess, preserve source references, flag uncertainty, and ask owners to validate outputs.

19
00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:03.180
The goal is risk reduction through better visibility, not unauthorized discovery or guesswork.
